\id TIT Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Titus
\toc1 The Epistle of Paul to Titus.
\toc2 Titus
\toc3 TIT
\mt1 The Epistle of Paul to Titus.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO TITUS.
\ip THAT Titus was a Gentil, and not a Iew, and that he was in S. Paules traine, at the leaſt the 14 yere after his conuerſion, if not before, we vnderſtand by the Epiſtle to the Galatians c. 2. And that he continued with him to the very end, appeareth in the ſecond to Timothee c. 4. Where he maketh mention that he ſent him from Rome into Dalmatia, when himſelf was ſhortly after to be put to death.
\ip And therefore although S. Luke neuer name him in the Actes, as neither himſelf, yet no doubt he comprehendeth him commonly, when he ſpeaketh thus in the firſt perſon plurall: \it Forthwith we ſought to goe into Macedonia. Act. 16.\it* For S. Paul alſo ſent him to Corinth, betwene the writing of his 1. & 2. to the Corinthians (which time concurreth with Act. 19) by occaſion whereof he maketh much and honorable mention of him in the ſaid ſecond Epiſtle c. 2. & c. 7. and againe he ſent him \f - \ft \+it 2. Corinth. 8.\+it*\f* with the ſame Epiſtle: both times about great matters: ſo that no doubt he was euen then alſo a Biſhop, and receiued accordingly of the Corinthians, \it with Feare and trembling.\it* 2. Cor. 7, v. 15. But the ſame is plainer in this Epiſtle to himſelf c. 1. v. 5. Where the Apoſtle ſaith: \it for this cauſe I left thee at Crete, &c.\it* By which wordes it is manifeſt alſo, that this Epiſtle was not written during the ſtorie of the Actes (ſeing that no mention is there of S. Paules being in the ile of Crete) but after his diſmiſſion at Rome out of his firſt trouble, and before his ſecond or laſt trouble there, as is euident by theſe wordes: \it When I ſhall ſend to thee Artemas or Tychicus, make haſt to come to me to Nicopolis, for there I haue determined to winter.\it* Tit. 3.
\ip Therefore he inſtructeth him (and in him, all Biſhops) much like as he doth Timothee, what qualities he muſt require in them that he ſhall make prieſts and Biſhops, in what ſort to preach, and to teach al ſortes of men, to commend good workes vnto them: finally, himſelf to be their example in all goodnes.
\c 1
\cd Of what qualitie the Prieſts and Biſhops muſt be: 9 namely learned, conſidering the Iudaical ſeducers of that time. 12 That the Cretenſians muſt be roughly vſed, to haue them continue ſound in faith.
\p
\v 1 \sc PAVL\sc* the ſeruant of God, and an Apoſtle of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt according to the faith of the elect of God and knowledge of the truth: vvhich is according to pietie
\v 2 into the hope of life euerlaſting, vvhich he promiſed that lieth not, God, \x - \xt 2. Tim. 1, 9.\x* before the ſecular times:
\v 3 but hath manifeſted in due times his vvord in preaching, vvhich is committed to me according to the precept of our Sauiour God:
\v 4 to Titus my beloued ſonne according to the common faith, grace and peace from God the father, and Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc* our Sauiour.
\v 5 For this cauſe left I thee in Crete, that thou ſhouldeſt reforme the things that are vvanting, and ſhouldeſt ordaine prieſtes by cities, as I alſo appointed thee: \ef - \fr 1.5 \fq Ordaine Prieſts. \ft ¹ Though Prieſts or Biſhops may be nominated and elected by the Princes, people, or Patrons of places, according to the vſe of the time and diuerſitie of Countries and faſhions, yet they can not be ordered and conſecrated but by a Biſhop who was him ſelf rightly ordered or conſecrated before, as this Titus vvas by S. Paul. And here it ſeemeth that he did not onely conſecrate them whom the people had elected before, but him ſelf alſo made choiſe of the perſons, no mention being here made of any other election popular. ² Which though it were long vſed in the primitiue Church, yet for diuers cauſes and ſpecially for continual tumultes, partialities, and diſorders which S. Auguſtine much complaineth of in his time, was iuſtly taken avvay, and other better meanes of their deſignement appointed. See \+it Conc. Laodic. cap. 12. 13\+it*. S. Auguſt. \+it de adult. coniug. li. 2. c. 20. Ep. 110. and Poſſid. in vita Aug. c. 8\+it*. ³ And that the ordering of Prieſts or impoſition of hands to that purpoſe, belongeth onely to Biſhops, and to no inferior Prieſts or other perſons, it is plaine by the Apoſtolike practiſe ſet downe in the Scriptures, namely in the Actes, and in the Epiſtles to Timothee and Titus. And S. Hierom, vvho ſeemeth ſometimes to ſay that in the primitiue Church there was no great difference betwixt a Biſhop and Prieſt, yet he euer excepteth giuing holy Orders, vvhich preeminence he attributeth to Biſhops onely. \+it ep. 85\+it*. as he doth alſo Confirming the Baptized by giuing them the holy Ghoſt through impoſition of hand and holy Chriſme. \+it Dial. cont. Lucifer. c. 4\+it*. ⁴ Note alſo that Aërius vvas of old condemned of hereſie, for holding that there vvas no difference betwixt a Prieſt and a Biſhop. \+it Epiph. hær. 75. Auguſt. hær. 53\+it*. ⁵ Note laſtly the fraudulent tranſlation of the Heretikes alvvaies, turning for \+it Prieſts\+it* (vvhich here is euident to be a calling of Order and office) \+it elders, ſaying, That thou ordaine Elders\+it*. which in our vulgar tonge ſignifieth the age, and not the Office properly: and al this for hatred of Prieſts. \fq ¹ \ft Priests must be consecrated by Bishops only. \fq ² \ft The popular election of the Cleargie taken away. \fq ³ \ft The preeminence of a Bishop aboue a Priest. \fq ⁴ \ft To put no difference betwene them is Aërius heresie. \fq ⁵ \ft Heret. translation.\ef*
\v 6 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 3, 2.\x* if any be vvithout crime, the huſband of one vvife, hauing faithful children, not in the accuſation of riote, or not obedient. \ef - \fr 1.6 \fq Of one vvife. \ft ¹ To that which is ſaid vpon the like vvordes \+it 1 Tim. 3\+it*: adde this teſtimonie of S. Epiphanius \+it li. 3. to. 2. cont. hæres. in fine. Holy Prieſthod\+it*, ſaith he, \+it for the moſt part procedeth of Virgins: and if not of virgins, yet of them that liue a ſole or ſingle life: but and if the ſingle and ſole perſons ſuffice not to the Miniſterie, of ſuch as conteine from their wiues, or after once marying remaine widowes. For, him that hath been maried tvviſe, it is not lawful to take to Prieſthod &c\+it*. If you liſt to ſee the cauſes why bigamie is forbidden them that are to be Prieſts, and continencie required of the Clergie, ſee the ſame author \+it li. 2. to. 1. hæres. 59\+it*. S. Ambroſe \+it li. 1. Offic. c. 50\+it*. and vpon \+it 1 Tim. 3\+it*. S. Auguſtine \+it de bono Coniugal. c. 18\+it*. S. Hierom \+it ep. 50 c. 5 ad Pammachium\+it*, and againſt Iouinian \+it li. 1 c. 19\+it*. S. Leo \+it ep. 87\+it*. and other auncient authors. ² And if the ſtudious reader peruſe al antiquitie, he ſhal finde al notable Biſhops and Prieſts of Gods Church to haue been ſingle, or continent from their vviues, if any were maried before they came to the Clergie. So was S. Paul, and exhorteth al men to the like: \+it 1 Cor. 7, 7\+it*. So vvere al the Apoſtles after they folovved Chriſt, as S. Hierom witneſſeth, affirming that our Lord loued Iohn ſpecially for his virginitie. \+it Apol. ad Pammach. c. 8. & li. 1 cont. Iouin. c. 14\+it*. S. Ignatius \+it ep. 6 ad Philadelph\+it*. ſaith of the ſaid Iohn, and of Timothee, Titus, Euodius, Clement, that they liued and died in chaſtitie, reckening vp of the old Teſtament diuers notable perſonages that did the ſame, as Elias, Ieſus Naue (otherwiſe called Ioſue) Melchiſedec, Eliſæus, Hieremie, Iohn Baptiſt. No man is ignorant that al the notable fathers of the Greeke and Latin Church liued chaſt: Athanaſius; Baſil, Nazianzene, Chryſoſtom, Cyprian, Hilarie (who entered into holy Orders after his vviues death) Ambroſe, Hierom, Auguſtine, Leo, Gregorie the great. Certaine other notable fathers had once wiues, but no holy men euer vſed them, much leſſe maried, after they vvere in holy Orders. ³ A maruelous thing, that ſo many heretofore ſhould haue the gift of chaſtitie then, and now ſo few, if the Proteſtants ſay true, that ſkarſe one among them in our age of al their ſectes, euen of their principal Superintendents, hath had it. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Bigami\+it* excluded from holy Orders, and the causes thereof. \fq ² \ft The notable men of both Testaments, that liued continently from wiues. \fq ³ \ft Only the Protestants complaine that they haue not the gift of chastitie.\ef*
\v 7 For a Biſhop muſt be vvithout crime, as the ſtevvard of God: not proud, not angrie, not giuen to vvine, no ſtriker, not couetous of filthy lucre:
\v 8 but giuen to hoſpitalitie, gentle, ſober, iuſt, holy, continent:
\v 9 embracing that faithful vvord vvhich is according to doctrine, that he may be able to exhort in ſound doctrine, & to reproue them that gaineſay it.
\v 10 For there be many diſobedient, vaine-ſpeakers, and ſeducers, eſpecially they that are of the Circumciſion.
\v 11 vvho muſt be controuled. vvho ſubuert vvhole houſes, teaching the things they ought not, for filthie lucre.
\v 12 One of them ſaid, their ovvne proper prophete, \f 1 \fr 1.12 \ft Epimenides.\f* \it The Cretenſians alvvaies liers, naughtie beaſtes, ſlouthful bellies\it*.
\v 13 This teſtimonie is true. For the vvhich cauſe rebuke them ſharpely, that they may be ſound in the faith,
\v 14 not attending to Ievviſh fables, and commaundements of men auerting them ſelues from the truth.
\v 15 \x - \xt Rom. 14, 20.\x* Al things are \f 2 \fr 1.15 \ft Rom. 14, 20. · He ſpeaketh not of the Churches abſtaining from meates ſome times, vvhich is not for any vncleannes in the creatures, but for chaſtening their bodies: but he meaneth the Iewiſh ſuperſtition, who now being Chriſtians, would not ceaſe to put difference of cleane and vncleane according to their old law. See S. Auguſtine Cont. Fauſt. li. 31. c. 4.\f* cleane to the cleane: but to the polluted and to infidels nothing is cleane: but polluted are both their minde and conſcience.
\v 16 They confeſſe that they knovv God: but in their vvorkes they deny, vvhereas they be abominable and incredulous and to euery good vvorke reprobate.
\c 2
\cd What to preach both to old and yong (not onely with word but vvith example alſo) and to ſeruants. 11 For there are of al ſortes in the Church, and they muſt be inſtructed accordingly.
\p
\v 1 \sc BVT\sc* doe thou ſpeake the things that become ſound doctrine.
\v 2 Old men that they be ſober, \f 1 \fr 2.2 \ft \+it σεμνοὺς pudicos\+it*\f* cchaſt, vviſe, ſound in the faith, in loue, in patience.
\v 3 Old women in like maner, in holy attire, not il ſpeakers, not giuen to much wine: teaching vvel,
\v 4 that they may teach the yong women vviſedom, to loue their huſbands, to loue their children,
\v 5 vviſe, chaſt, ſober, hauing a care of the houſe, gentle, ſubiect to their huſbandes, that the vvord of God be not blaſphemed.
\v 6 Yong men in like maner exhort that they be ſober.
\v 7 In al things ſhevv thy ſelf an example of good vvorkes, in doctrine, in integritie, in grauitie,
\v 8 the vvord ſound, irreprehenſible: that he vvhich is on the contrarie part, may be afraid, hauing no euil to ſay of vs.
\v 9 \x - \xt Eph. 6, 5. Col. 3, 22. 1. Pet. 2, 18.\x* Seruants to be ſubiect to their maiſters, in al things pleaſing, not gainſaying:
\v 10 not defrauding, but in al things ſhewing good faith, that they may adorne the doctrine of our Sauiour God in al things.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 2.11 \ft The Epiſtle at the firſt Maſſe on Chriſtmas day, and vpon the Circumciſion of our Lord.\f* For the grace of God our Sauiour hath appeared to al men:
\v 12 inſtructing vs that denying impietie & worldly deſires, vve liue ſoberly, and iuſtly, and godly in this vvorld,
\v 13 expecting the bleſſed hope and \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft \+it c ἐπιφὰνειαν\+it*\f* caduent of the glorie of the great God and our Sauiour \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt,
\v 14 vvho gaue him ſelf for vs, that he might redeeme vs from al iniquitie, and might cleanſe to him ſelf a people acceptable, a purſuer of good vvorkes.
\v 15 Theſe things ſpeake, and exhort ⊢ and rebuke \f 1 \fr 2.15 \ft Biſhops muſt be ſtout and cōmaund in Gods cauſe, and the people muſt in no vviſe diſobey or contemne them.\f* vvith al authoritie. Let no man contemne thee.
\c 3
\cd To teach them obedience vnto Princes, and meekenes tovvardes al men, conſidering that we alſo were as they, til God of his goodnes brought vs to Baptiſme. 8 To teach good vvorkes, 9 and to auoid vaine queſtions, 10 and obſtinate Heretikes.
\p
\v 1 \sc ADMONISH\sc* them to be ſubiect to Princes and Poteſtates, to obey at a vvord, to be ready to euery good vvorke,
\v 2 to blaſpheme no man, not to be litigious, but modeſt: ſhevving al mildenes tovvard al men.
\v 3 for we alſo vvere ſometime vnwiſe, incredulous, erring, ſeruing diuerſe deſires & voluptuouſneſſes, liuing in malice & enuie, odible, hating one an other.
\v 4 But when \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft The Epiſtle at the 2 Maſſe on Chriſtmas day, and in the Votiue Maſſe of our B. Lady betwene Chriſtmas and Candlemas.\f* the benignitie and \f 2 \fr 3.4 \ft \+it c φιλανθρωπία\+it*\f* ckindnes tovvard man of our Sauiour God appeared:
\v 5 \x - \xt 2. Tim. 1, 9.\x* not by the vvorkes of iuſtice vvhich vve did, but according to his mercie he hath ſaued vs \f 2 \fr 3.5 \ft As before in the Sacrament of holy Orders ( \+it 1. Tim. 4. and 2 Tim. 1\+it* ) ſo here it is plaine that Baptiſme giueth grace, & that by it as by an inſtrumental cauſe we be ſaued.\f* by the lauer of regeneratiō and renouation of the holy Ghoſt,
\v 6 vvhom he hath povvred vpon vs aboundantly by \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt our Sauiour:
\v 7 that being iuſtified by his grace, vve may be heires according to hope of life euerlaſting. ⊢
\v 8 \x - \xt 1. Tim. 4.\x* It is a faithful ſaying, and of theſe things I vvil haue thee auouch earneſtly: that they vvhich beleeue in God, be careful to excell in good vvorkes. Theſe things be good and profitable for men.
\v 9 But \x - \xt 2. Tim. 2, 23.\x* fooliſh queſtions, and genealogies, and contentions, and controuerſies of the Lavv auoid. For they are vnprofitable and vaine.
\v 10 A man that is an heretike after the firſt and ſecond \f 1 \fr 3.10 \ft Theſe admonitions or correptions muſt be giuen to ſuch as erre, by our Spiritual Gouernours and Paſtors: to whom if they yeld not, Chriſtian men muſt auoid them.\f* admonition auoid: \ef - \fr 3.10 \fq A man that is an Heretike. \ft ¹ Not euery one that erreth in religion, is an Heretike, but he onely that after the Churches determination, wilfully and ſtubburnely ſtandeth in his falſe opinion, not yelding to decree of Councel or the cheefe Paſtors of the Church therein. \+it They\+it* (ſaith S. Auguſtine \+it ep. 162\+it*.) \+it that defend their ſentence (though falſe and peruerſe) with no ſtubburne ſtomake or obſtinate hart, ſpecially if it be ſuch as them ſelues by bold preſumption broched not, but receiued it of their deceiued parents, and do ſeeke the truth vvarily and carefully, being ready to be reformed if they finde it, ſuch are not to be reputed among Heretikes\+it*. And againe, \+it li. 18 de Ciuit. c. 51\+it*. ² \+it They that in the Church of Chriſt haue any craſed or peruerſe opinion, if being admoniſhed to be of a ſound and right opinion, they reſiſt obſtinatly, and vvil not amend their peſtiferous opinions, but perſiſt in defenſe of them, are thereby become Heretikes, and going forth out of the Church, are counted for enimies that exerciſe vs\+it*. Againe \+it li. 4 de Bapt. cont. Donat. c. 16. He is an Heretike that, when the doctrine of the Catholike faith is made plaine and manifeſt vnto him, had rather reſiſt it, and chooſe that vvhich him ſelf held &c\+it*. And in diuers places he declareth that S. Cyprian, though he held an errour, yet vvas no Heretike, becauſe he vvould not haue defended it after a general Council had declared it to be an errour. \+it li. 2 de bapt. c. 4\+it*. So Poſſidonius in the life of S. Auguſtine ³ reporteth, how, after the determination of the See Apoſtolike that Pelagius opinion vvas heretical, al men eſteemed Pelagius an Heretike, and the Emperour made lavves againſt him as againſt an Heretike. Againe S. Auguſtine ſaith, \+it He is an Heretike in my opinion, that for ſome temporal commoditie, and ſpecially for his glorie and principalitie, coineth or els foloweth falſe or new opinions\+it*. de vtilit. credendi cap. 1. ⁴ Let our Proteſtants behold them ſelues in this glaſſe, and vvithal let them marke al other proprieties that old Heretikes euer had, and they ſhal finde al definitions and markes of an Heretike to fall vpon them ſelues. And therfore they muſt not maruel if vve warne al Catholike men by the vvordes of the Apoſtle in this place, to take heede of them, and to ſhunne their preachings, bookes, conuenticles and companies. ⁵ Neither neede the people be curious to knowe what they ſay, much leſſe to confute them: but they muſt truſt Gods Church, which doth refute and condemne them. And it is ynough for them to knowe that they be condemned, as S Auguſtine noteth in the later end of his booke de hereſibus. And S. Cyprian ⁶ ſaith notably to Antonianus demaunding curiouſly vvhat hereſies Nouatianus did teach: \+it No matter\+it*, quoth he, \+it What hereſies he hath or teacheth, when he teacheth vvithout\+it*, that is to ſay, out of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Who is properly an Heretike, & who is not. \fq ² \ft Descriptions or markes how to know an Heretike. \fq ³ \ft \+it Vit. Aug. c. 18\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft The former markes agree to the Protestants. \fq ⁵ \ft Their bookes, seruice, & preaching must be auoided. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Ep. 52. nu. 7\+it*.\ef*
\v 11 knovving that he that is ſuch an one, is ſubuerted, and ſinneth, being condemned by his ovvne iudgement. \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq Subuerted. \ft ¹ Heretikes are often incorrigible, yet the Church of God ceaſeth not by al meanes poſſible to reuoke them. therfore S. Auguſtine ſaith \+it ep. 162. The Heretike him ſelf though ſwelling with odious and deteſtable pride, and madde vvith the frovvardnes of wicked contention, as We admoniſh that he be auoided leſt he deceiue the vveaklings and litle ones, ſo we refuse not by al meanes poſſible to ſeeke his amendment and reformation\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Church seeketh the amendement of the most obstinate Heretikes.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq By his owne iudgement. \ft ¹ Other greuous offenders be ſeparated by excommunication from the communion of Saincts and the fellowſhip of Gods Church, by the ſentence of their Superiors in the ſame Church: but Heretikes more miſerable and infortunate then they, runne out of the Church of their ovvne accord, and ſo giue ſentence againſt their ovvne ſoules to damnation. \fq ¹ \ft Heretikes cut them selues frō the Church.\ef*
\v 12 VVhen I ſhal ſend to thee Artemas or Tychicus, haſten to come vnto me to Nicopolis. for there I haue determined to vvinter.
\v 13 Set forvvard Zenas the lavvyer and Apollos carefully, that nothing be vvanting to them.
\v 14 And let our men alſo learne \f 1 \fr 3.14 \ft c προίςασθαι præeſſe.\f* to excel in good vvorkes to neceſſarie vſes: that they be not vnfruiteful.
\v 15 Al that are vvith me, ſalute thee: ſalute them that loue vs in the faith. The grace of God be vvith you al. Amen.
